ider the tail sometimes you  don't but that's that's really really it  so  about 125 000 atoms i've been doing some  backing literally back where's my  envelope purposely yeah literally back  of the envelope calculation so we do a  lot of this we do a lot of mucking  around in back of an envelopes on backs  of envelopes we actually use the back of  an envelope i didn't age yes because it  was the closest thing to hand and then  when you work out in terms of the volume  of this compared to this you'd get 10 to  the 18 which is uh one quintillion of  these into this volume brady continually  tells me stop talking in terms of 10 to  the 18 and 10 to the 19 and 10 to the  bloody blood means nothing and it  absolutely doesn't mean anything but um  it turns out that uh if you wanted to  have 10 to the 18 of these how much  volume would that take up well again  back in the envelope calculation it  would you'd require a 20 meter deep pile  of  maltesers which spread out over an area  the size of ireland and for those of you  on the other side of the big pond  ireland is about the same area as the  state of indiana as well as sort of  seeing the faces these lines are lattice  planes and they arise from  the wavelength of the  electrons that we're using to make this  image is thus that  they uh diffract from the crystal planes  and those diffraction just the same ways  if you take a cd  made of a cd  queens of the stone edge  the colors that you get  so so the rainbow color that comes from  the fraction that comes because the size  of the of the features on the disc  that's used to encode the information  are roughly the same size as the  wavelength of light now what you  basically have here is an atomic scale  dvd or atomic scale cd and now instead  of bouncing light off it you're bouncing  electron waves and in this case the  electron wave  wavelength is roughly as is comparable  to the spacing of the planes and you end  up seeing the diffraction from those  planes in the i